## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to respond to production incidents with a concrete, beginner-friendly plan focusing on observable signals, containment, rollback, and validation under time pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Observability: latency p95, error rate, queue depth, redis/backlog metrics
- Containment: throttle concurrency, pause new work, revert patch safely
- Rollback & Validation: restore previous config, synthetic tests, end-to-end checks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple end-to-end validation stub
async function checkDelivery(queueClient, producer) {
  const msg = {id: 'test', payload: 'ping'};
  await producer.send(msg);
  const ok = await queueClient.awaitDeliveryAck(msg.id, 5000);
  return ok;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate the rollback trigger and rollback-on-fail thresholds?
- Which metrics would you add to distinguish transient spikes from persistent issues?

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges ability to manage per-tenant QoS under bursty traffic and to design practical safeguards that minimize blast radius.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant QoS and quotas
- Backpressure and prioritization
- Isolation and graceful degradation
- Telemetry, SLO-driven rollback

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, burst) {
    this.rate = rate; this.burst = burst; this.tokens = burst; this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.burst, this.tokens + delta * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for a service mesh with mutual TLS and header-based routing?
- How would you test failover and simulate tenant backoffs in a staging environment?
